    "markdown": "# Negotiation and Conflict Management\n\n\n\n::: {.callout-note}\nThis chapter is in preparation. It will be built from **Lecture 10 --\nNegotiation and Conflict Management** of MGMT-3105 (and its answer deck),\nfollowing the same structure as [Chapter 1](01-Sustainability.qmd) and\n[Chapter 9](09-Cost-Estimation-and-Budgeting.qmd).\n:::\n\n## Learning objectives (planned)\n\nBy the end of this chapter you will be able to:\n\n- Explain why conflict is normal on projects and distinguish functional from dysfunctional conflict.\n- Apply the Thomas-Kilmann modes (competing, collaborating, compromising, avoiding, accommodating) to a situation.\n- Prepare for a negotiation: interests vs. positions, BATNA, ZOPA, reservation and target points.\n- Apply principled negotiation (separate people from problem; focus on interests; invent options; use objective criteria).\n- Manage conflict with suppliers, functional managers, and team members.\n- Recognise cultural and ethical dimensions of negotiation in a global supply chain.\n\n## Topic outline\n\n1. Sources of project conflict across the life cycle.\n2. Conflict-handling modes and when each is appropriate.\n3. Distributive vs. integrative negotiation.\n4. Preparation: BATNA, ZOPA, anchoring.\n5. Principled negotiation.\n6. Difficult conversations and escalation paths.\n7. Running case: negotiating the motor/control-system supply contract for the RC-vehicle project.\n",
